  "id": "Compound::DB01213 - Gene::124",
  "verbalization": "Fomepizole is shown to interact with Adh1a through a series of molecular and functional relationships. The evidence suggests that Fomepizole binds to Adh1b, which is a subunit of alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and is involved in the zinc-dependent activity of ADH. This activity is then linked to Adh1a, indicating that Adh1a is a target of the ADH activity mediated by Adh1b. The binding of Fomepizole to Adh1b may inhibit the enzymatic activity of Adh1a, thereby affecting the metabolism of alcohol. The consistent evidence across the three paths—whether Adh1b or Adh1c is the binding site—suggests that Fomepizole's interaction with Adh1a is mediated through the ADH pathway, with Adh1a being a downstream target of the ADH activity. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of ADH in alcohol metabolism and the potential of Fomepizole as an inhibitor of this process.",
